### What is stream laziness?

Streams are lazy because intermediate operations do nothing when called. They only register a stage in the pipeline descriptor. Execution happens only when a terminal operation is invoked.

### How can you prove laziness?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class LazinessProof {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stream<String> pipeline = List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002", "CUR3001").stream()
            .filter(a -> { System.out.println("filter: " + a); return a.startsWith("SAV"); })
            .map(a -> { System.out.println("map: " + a); return a.toLowerCase(); });

        System.out.println("Pipeline built. No output yet.");
        System.out.println("--- Terminal triggers ---");

        List<String> result = pipeline.toList();
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Pipeline built. No output yet.
--- Terminal triggers ---
filter: SAV2001
map: SAV2001
filter: SAV2002
map: SAV2002
filter: CUR3001
Result: [sav2001, sav2002]
```

No filter or map executed until toList() triggered the pipeline.

### What are short-circuit operations?

Short-circuit intermediates truncate the stream. Short-circuit terminals stop processing entirely.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class ShortCircuitDemo {
    record Transaction(String id, BigDecimal amount, boolean settled) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Transaction> txns = List.of(
            new Transaction("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000"), false),
            new Transaction("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("45000"), true),
            new Transaction("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("78000"), true),
            new Transaction("TXN-004", new BigDecimal("120000"), true)
        );

        // findFirst: stops after first match
        txns.stream()
            .peek(t -> System.out.println("Checking: " + t.id()))
            .filter(Transaction::settled)
            .findFirst()
            .ifPresent(t -> System.out.println("Found: " + t.id()));

        System.out.println("---");

        // limit: stops after 2 settled
        txns.stream()
            .filter(Transaction::settled)
            .peek(t -> System.out.println("Passed: " + t.id()))
            .limit(2)
            .forEach(t -> System.out.println("Result: " + t.id()));
    }
}
```

```text
Checking: TXN-001
Checking: TXN-002
Found: TXN-002
---
Passed: TXN-002
Result: TXN-002
Passed: TXN-003
Result: TXN-003
```

findFirst stopped after TXN-002. limit(2) stopped after the second settled transaction. TXN-004 was never processed.

### What is loop fusion?

Fusion means multiple stateless operations execute in a single pass per element, not separate iterations over the entire stream.

### How do laziness, fusion, and short-circuit combine?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.Stream;
import java.util.List;

public class CombinedOptimisations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 10 million elements, but limit(3) means only a few are processed
        List<String> result = Stream.iterate(1, n -> n + 1)
            .peek(n -> System.out.println("Generated: " + n))
            .filter(n -> n % 1000 == 0)
            .map(n -> "TXN-" + n)
            .limit(3)
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Result: " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Generated: 1
Generated: 2
...
Generated: 1000
Generated: 1001
...
Generated: 2000
...
Generated: 3000
Result: [TXN-1000, TXN-2000, TXN-3000]
```

Only 3,000 elements were generated from an infinite source. The remaining elements were never created.

### What happens without a terminal?

```java
// This pipeline does NOTHING: no terminal!
List.of("SAV2001", "SAV2002").stream()
    .filter(a -> a.startsWith("SAV"))
    .peek(System.out::println); // No output! peek is intermediate!
// The pipeline compiles and runs, but produces zero output.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Laziness is what makes streams composable: you can build pipeline fragments, pass them around, and only execute when ready. The JVM's Spliterator engine drives pull-based execution internally. For parallel streams, laziness enables each ForkJoinPool thread to process its partition independently before merging results.
